Part of Cloudberry's isolation2_schedule on the port's cluster: the
differences from Cloudberry's expected output that have been reviewed, and
are the port's for a reason of its own.

Each file is one test's difference in the form ../../singlenode/canon.pl
gives it, named for the expected output it differs from; a name with a pass
in it (test.orca.diff) holds for that pass alone.  run.sh counts a test as
passing when gpdiff.pl, under Cloudberry's init_file and the port's, finds no
difference or exactly the file's.

add_column_after_vacuum_skip_drop_column, vacuum_after_vacuum_skip_drop_column
  A column table's segment file that a VACUUM compacted while another
  session held the table: Cloudberry's VACUUM drops such a file in a phase
  that needs the table's AccessExclusiveLock and, failing to take it, leaves
  the file awaiting drop (state 2) beside the one it compacted into, for an
  ADD COLUMN or the next VACUUM to clean up -- what the two tests are for.
  gp_ao recycles a compacted file once no snapshot can see what it held,
  under no lock of the table's (ao_vacuum.c, ao_recycle()), and session 1,
  whose SELECT has ended, holds the table but no snapshot: the file is
  recycled as the VACUUM ends, back in state 1 and empty, so none awaits
  drop.  And the file it compacts into is taken only when a live row moves,
  so a file whose rows are all deleted leaves no second one.  The INSERT
  after goes into segment file 1, as both tests want.

bitmap_index_concurrent
  EXPLAIN's four plans and nothing else.  Session 1 plans with PostgreSQL's
  planner in either pass, whose route reads the table's rows through a
  Gather Motion of the table -- of segment 0 alone, where the key or
  gp_segment_id = 0 fixes it -- and each segment plans its own scan of them,
  where Cloudberry's plan shows its Bitmap Heap Scan or Index Scan under the
  Motion.  The segment's scan is the bitmap index's, as Cloudberry's is: the
  test's faults hold it after it read a bitmap page
  (after_read_one_bitmap_idx_page) while an INSERT rearranges the page's
  words onto the next (rearrange_word_to_next_bitmap_page), and the counts
  it then gives, which the test is for, are Cloudberry's.

bitmap_index_inspect
  What the index's pages hold, and nothing else.  The port's pages are
  PostgreSQL's 8 kB, where Cloudberry builds with 32 kB (configure's
  --with-blocksize), so each value's 1000 rows of the table fill heap pages
  of 226 rows where Cloudberry's fill pages of 909: each value's last TIDs
  in its list-of-values item (bm_lov_page_items()), and its bitmap page --
  for each heap page a fill word of ones, the literal word of the rest and
  a fill word of zeros (bm_bitmap_page_header(), bm_bitmap_page_items()) --
  are those of 8 kB pages.  The functions are Cloudberry's pageinspect's,
  gp_ao's here (bitmap/bitmapinspect.c): the meta page, which pages are
  which, and the errors are Cloudberry's.

concurrent_partition_table_operations_should_not_deadlock, and its
_optimizer output, which the orca pass is compared with
  EXPLAIN's plans and nothing else: the operations the test runs side by
  side do not deadlock.  The planner reads each partition through a Gather
  Motion of its own under an Append, where Cloudberry gathers one Append
  from the segments; ORCA's Dynamic Scan is shown with the scan it runs on
  each partition beneath it.  For the query the index covers, ORCA picks the
  index-only scan Cloudberry's picks, since VACUUM brings back the segments'
  all-visible pages to the coordinator, as Cloudberry's does (vacuum.c).

distributed_snapshot  four things of Cloudberry's that the port does not
  have, and nothing of the distributed snapshots the test is for.
  test_consume_xids is a function of Cloudberry's regress.so, which is not
  built for the port, so the scenario that burns transaction IDs up to a
  segment of the distributed log burns none.  gp_distributed_log is the view
  of that log; the port keeps what it needs of it -- which prepared part
  stands for which distributed transaction -- in shared memory, pruned as
  distributed snapshots advance (gp_internal.dtx_map()), and has no files to
  truncate.  ALTER TABLE ... EXPAND TABLE is M7's, so the rows of a table on
  two of the three segments stay where they are, and the SELECT after it
  shows them there.  And Debug_print_full_dtm is a setting of Cloudberry's,
  whose LOG lines the port does not print.

execute_on_utilitymode, in each pass a file of its own
  A session of the coordinator's own (-1U), an ordinary one here, as the
  port's coordinator has no utility mode: a function EXECUTE ON ALL SEGMENTS
  runs on the segments, foo and bar of each, where Cloudberry's utility mode
  runs it on the node it is connected to, foo -1 and bar -1.  And in the
  orca pass, the function EXECUTE ON ANY IMMUTABLE: ORCA runs it where its
  plan of the join puts it, on each segment, which keeps the rows that hash
  to it (a Result's Hash Filter), as for any immutable function -- this one
  reads gp_contentid, whose value an immutable function may not depend on,
  so bar 0 and bar 1 come back.  Its other cases, and every case in a
  session of a segment's own (1U), run on the node connected to, as
  Cloudberry's.

export_distributed_snapshot
  The distributed fields of an exported snapshot, which the port's file has
  none of: the coordinator's snapshot is the distributed one, sent with each
  statement, so a transaction that imports it reads what its exporter reads
  on every segment, as the test's cases of INSERT, DELETE, UPDATE and DROP
  show.  So the case that corrupts dsxmin corrupts nothing, and SET
  TRANSACTION SNAPSHOT takes the file, where Cloudberry's finds it invalid;
  and a session of the coordinator's own (-1U), an ordinary one here, imports
  the snapshot a distributed session exported, which Cloudberry's utility
  mode refuses.

fts_errors
  Two of its sessions after content 1 is failed over, where the port's
  dispatch differs, and nothing of FTS's: its name that does not resolve
  (get_dns_cached_address) fails FTS's round and marks no segment down, as
  Cloudberry's; the transaction that ends on the old primary fails and asks
  FTS to probe, which fails content 1 over; a session outside a
  transaction goes to the new primary; one with temporary tables is told
  its gang was lost; and one in a subtransaction fails its statement and
  its ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT, as Cloudberry's do.  The session whose cursor
  is open: Cloudberry's FETCH returns what its segments sent as the cursor
  was declared, none, and its END finds the gang lost; the port's cursor
  reads from the segments as it is fetched from, and its FETCH meets the
  old primary's closed connection.  And the session with temporary tables,
  on its next statement: Cloudberry's forgets them as the gang goes, and
  the name does not resolve; the port's coordinator drops them as that
  statement's executor starts, after its name was resolved, and the
  statement finds no relation by the OID it was planned with, which the
  port's init_file masks -- in both passes alike, where the segments were
  asked before: the other primaries' backends had dropped the table as they
  ended, and the new primary's catalog still had it without its file, and
  under ORCA whichever answered first was the error.

gdd/concurrent_update, and its _optimizer output, which the orca pass is
compared with
  Two things.  PostgreSQL 19's words for a row another transaction's Split
  moved away: a DELETE, an UPDATE and a SELECT ... FOR UPDATE that meet it
  fail, as Cloudberry's do, "tuple to be locked was already moved to
  another partition due to concurrent update", which Cloudberry's own
  heapam_handler.c reworded to "another partition or segment".  A Split
  deletes a row it moves as PostgreSQL deletes one an UPDATE moves to
  another partition (gp_split.c).  And the "Test EvalplanQual" section,
  whose sessions plan with PostgreSQL's planner in either pass: its UPDATE
  ... FROM of two randomly distributed tables, which waited on a segment
  for the row the other session updated, rechecks the row's new version as
  PostgreSQL's does, and updates it -- UPDATE 1, and the row (3, 1) --
  where Cloudberry's recheck meets the Motion below its write and fails,
  "EvalPlanQual can not handle subPlan with Motion node" (gp_explicit.c's
  explicit_requalify()).  The test's two fault sections pass as they are:
  the coordinator is held as its transaction ends for the other sessions,
  after its parts committed (before_xact_end_procarray, in gp_dispatch.c's
  O33 hook), and a segment's writer as it looks up the distributed
  transactions of the local ones it may wait for, in its map
  (before_get_distributed_xid, gp_dtx.c).

gdd/insert_root_partition_truncate_deadlock, in the planner pass alone
  No deadlock forms for the detector to break.  The planner's route writes
  an INSERT's rows from the coordinator, which makes them all and then
  sends each segment its share as a COPY, one segment after another
  (gp_modify.c), where Cloudberry's segments run the INSERT's slice at
  once: segment 0's COPY is held where its part begins (func_init_plan_end),
  before it routes a row to a partition, and segments 1 and 2 have not
  begun theirs, so the TRUNCATE of the leaf takes its lock on every node at
  once and ends -- the driver finds it not blocking, and has nothing to
  wait for after -- and the INSERT then writes its 30 rows.  Under ORCA,
  whose INSERT runs on every segment at once, segments 1 and 2 hold the
  leaf, the INSERT waits on segment 0 for the TRUNCATE that waits for it on
  the others, and the global deadlock detector cancels the TRUNCATE, as
  Cloudberry's does.

lockmodes, and its _optimizer output, which the orca pass is compared with
  Three things, none of them a lock of Cloudberry's the port does not take,
  on heap and append-optimized tables alike -- on the latter, with the
  detector on too, the ExclusiveLock a locking clause takes where no row of
  theirs can be locked, as Cloudberry's parser takes it (gp_modify.c,
  segments_lock_rows()).  Every lock Cloudberry holds on the
  coordinator is held, as it takes them (O30): without the global deadlock
  detector the ExclusiveLock as the parser opens the table, and with it,
  for a locking clause, AccessShareLock from the parser and then
  RowShareLock or ExclusiveLock -- as Cloudberry's parser holds them, the
  port's taken as the query is planned.  What differs beside them is
  incidental: without the detector Cloudberry's parser looks a table a
  locking clause names up under AccessShareLock first, where the port takes
  the ExclusiveLock alone; the port prints an UPDATE or DELETE for the
  segments, and PostgreSQL's ruleutils takes AccessShareLock on what it
  prints; a cached upsert's EXCLUDED entry asks for RowExclusiveLock,
  after the target's ExclusiveLock; and ORCA, which plans an UPDATE of a
  partitioned table here where Cloudberry's leaves it to the planner, reads
  each partition's constraint, which PostgreSQL builds under an
  AccessShareLock on the table, one lock more beside its ExclusiveLock.  The planner's plans are the port's:
  each table gathered from the segments and joined on the coordinator, and
  with the detector on the rows of a locking clause locked on the segments
  by the gather's query, where Cloudberry's plan has a LockRows.  And
  pg_stats is PostgreSQL 19's, of five columns more, whose tableid the
  port's init_file masks: the root's statistics are Cloudberry's, merged
  from its leaves' as the test's fault waits (gp_partmerge.c), and the
  leaf's correlation is the port's, over the sample as it arrived, where
  Cloudberry's is the mean of each segment's.

modify_table_data_corrupt, and its _optimizer output, which the orca pass
is compared with
  The plans, and the outcome of the two statements the test expects the
  planner to fail.  The planner's route gathers the join's tables to the
  coordinator and sends each row's DELETE or UPDATE back to the segment it
  was read on, by its gp_segment_id (Custom Scan (Explicit Redistribute
  Motion), gp_explicit.c), where Cloudberry's planner redistributes the
  rows by the table's key: the row segment 0 holds where its key does not
  hash is changed where it is, with the other, DELETE 2 and UPDATE 2.
  Under ORCA, whose plans are Cloudberry's, each row reaches the segment
  its key hashes to, and the check of the segment it was read on fails the
  three statements the test expects ORCA to fail, in Cloudberry's words
  (gp_internal.check_row_segment(), CTranslatorDXLToPlStmt.cpp).  ORCA's
  plans show the port's Dispatch above the ModifyTable, which it runs on
  the segments, the slices numbered from it, no Result of a One-Time Filter
  above the split update, and a hash key qualified with its table, as an
  ORCA plan's EXPLAIN qualifies its columns here (see spilling_hashagg).

segwalrep/commit_blocking_on_standby
  SHOW's column of repl_catchup_within_range, which is the setting's name:
  the port's is gp.repl_catchup_within_range, three characters longer, so
  the header and its rule are too.  The setting is Cloudberry's: 1 WAL
  segment, within which the coordinator waits for a standby that is catching
  up (gp_standby.c), and FTS turns a primary's synchronous replication on
  for a mirror that is (gp_fts.c).

segwalrep/die_commit_pending_replication
  Its one-phase half alone: the port commits in one phase only a segment's
  part that wrote, as it prepares only one that wrote (gp_dtx.c), where
  Cloudberry commits in one phase, on every segment it reached, a
  transaction whose segments wrote nothing (prepareDtxTransaction(),
  cdbtm.c).  So the test's transaction, whose segments only read, reaches
  no segment's one-phase commit, nor the fault the test puts there,
  start_performDtxProtocolCommitOnePhase, and its END commits.  The half the
  test is named for is as Cloudberry's: a segment's backend terminated as
  its COMMIT PREPARED waits for the mirror, the coordinator tells the
  segment again over a new connection, whose COMMIT PREPARED finds the part
  committed and waits for the mirror to have it, as Cloudberry's does
  (gp_dispatch.c, dtx_finish_again()), and the INSERT commits.

segwalrep/fts_unblock_primary
  Where Cloudberry's fault initialize_wal_sender holds the mirror's WAL
  sender: in PostgreSQL 19 no hook runs in a WAL sender after it has
  registered, so the port holds it where the connection is authenticated
  (gp_fts.c), before it is one, and gp_stat_replication shows no state for
  it where Cloudberry's shows startup.  What the test is for holds: FTS
  reports the mirror down while its WAL sender is held, and up and in sync
  once it goes on.

segwalrep/mirror_promotion
  Its tablespace, not its failovers: the port does not dispatch CREATE
  TABLESPACE -- a tablespace is a directory, and the segments of one host
  would share it -- so the table the test puts in one is refused on the
  segments, and its DROP finds nothing.  Both failovers, the one from a
  primary stopped and the one from a primary that does not answer FTS in
  time, and both full recoveries, are as Cloudberry's.

oom_startup_memory  what the memory a process starts with -- 12 MB, which
  each process of a session on a segment takes from the session's and the
  node's limits as it connects (memprot.c) -- does to the test's two
  scenarios, which are the port's dispatch's rather than Cloudberry's.  Its
  join of four gp_dist_random('gp_id') runs on the coordinator, which reads
  each through the session's one connection to each segment, so that no
  segment starts a second process for the session and the session stays
  within the per-query limit, 20 MB, where Cloudberry's four slices start
  four and the second is refused; the 81 rows are three segments' to the
  fourth power.  And the eight sessions' SET of a setting: the port sends a
  setting to the segments with the next statement that goes to them, and a
  SET goes to none, so no session starts a process on a segment and none
  runs past the node's limit, 60 MB, where Cloudberry's SET opens each
  session's processes on the segments and the sixth is refused.  The
  refusals themselves are Cloudberry's, in its words, and the memory
  protection suite makes them happen (../memprot/run.sh).

pg_views_concurrent_drop  a view dropped in one session while another
  reads its definition from pg_views: Greenplum made the definition come
  back NULL (MPP-25160, try_relation_open in ruleutils.c's make_viewdef),
  and PostgreSQL raises "could not open relation with OID ...", which the
  test's own comment says.  The port runs PostgreSQL 19's ruleutils.c, which
  no extension can change, so the second session gets PostgreSQL's error, as
  singlenode_isolation2's does.  The OID is masked by ../init_file.

prepared_xact_deadlock_pg_rewind
  The CREATE DATABASE whose part on segment 0 is prepared as the primary
  goes down fails in PostgreSQL's words for a connection that closed, where
  Cloudberry's add "Error on receive from" the segment (as in
  parallel_retrieve_cursor/status_check, below), and nothing else differs:
  the part is rolled back on the mirror FTS promoted, and gprecoverseg
  rewinds the old primary, whose crash recovery gpsegrewind.py finishes in
  database postgres, as Cloudberry's pg_rewind does, where PostgreSQL 19's
  would wait in template1 for the lock the recovered CREATE DATABASE holds.

reader_waits_for_lock, in the planner pass alone
  No reader waits, because the port's planner has none: it reads each
  distributed table of the join through a gather of its own, on the
  segments' writers, and joins on the coordinator, where Cloudberry's
  planner makes a slice for each table, run by readers.  So the writer waits
  for the lock the utility session holds, check_readers_are_blocked() finds
  no reader among the waiters after its two minutes, and the query of them
  finds no row.  Under ORCA the reader and the writer each wait, and the
  test passes.

resource_queue  what pg_stat_activity and pg_locks say of a backend that
  waits for a resource queue's slot: its wait event, which gp_resource
  names ResourceQueue, is an extension's, of type Extension, where
  Cloudberry's has a type of its own; and pg_locks lists no queue.
  Cloudberry's queue is a lock of a lock method of its own, and PostgreSQL
  19's lock methods are fixed, so a slot is held and waited for in
  gp_resource's shared memory (resqueue.c), which
  gp_toolkit.gp_locks_on_resqueue reads.  The waits themselves -- two
  EXECUTEs held until the cursor's transaction ends, then each let through
  -- are Cloudberry's.

runaway_query, in the planner pass alone  EXPLAIN's plan of the query ORCA
  fails on, which the planner makes: the port's planner sends a query whose
  distribution key its condition fixes to the one segment that holds the
  key, and EXPLAIN prints the Gather Motion that reads it there, with the
  relation and the segment, where Cloudberry's shows a Seq Scan and its
  Filter under the Motion.  Under ORCA the fault fails the query, as
  Cloudberry's does.

spilling_hashagg, and its _optimizer output, which the orca pass is
compared with
  EXPLAIN's plans alone; what the aggregates answer is Cloudberry's.  The
  planner's route gathers the table and aggregates on the coordinator, a
  GroupAggregate over a Sort, where Cloudberry's planner aggregates on the
  segments, hashed in two stages.  ORCA's plans are Cloudberry's, but
  EXPLAIN qualifies each column with its table -- "Group Key:
  test_src_tbl.a" -- because an ORCA plan's range table has the query's own
  entries beside its scans', for the executor's privilege checks
  (add_query_permissions_walker(), orca.c), and PostgreSQL's EXPLAIN
  qualifies the columns of a plan of more than one entry.  The fault the
  test sets, force_hashagg_stream_hashtable, is in no code of Cloudberry's
  either, and is never hit.

starve_case
  PostgreSQL's words for the error of function_starve_volatile(): it runs in
  the coordinator's own backend, where Cloudberry runs it in an entry-db
  reader, so the error says where in the function's query it failed -- LINE
  and QUERY -- and names the function with its argument types.  That the
  reader's lock waits behind the waiting AccessExclusiveLock, which is what
  the test is for, holds, each session found waiting by its gp_session_id
  in pg_locks.mppsessionid.

terminate_in_gang_creation
  The first INSERT of a session after every primary restarted, which fails,
  and the next succeeds, as Cloudberry's: in PostgreSQL's words for the
  session's connection the restart closed, which the coordinator meets as it
  sends the INSERT's rows to the segments over it -- the planner's VALUES are
  the coordinator's own, and there is no reader to make -- where Cloudberry's
  makes a reader gang for its VALUES, whose new process does not find its
  writer.  The backend terminated while its gang is made ends with nothing
  to free, as the test is for.


Cloudberry's parallel_retrieve_cursor_schedule, in the group retrieve.  Its
cursors are ORCA's in both passes (gp_endpoint.c), so each difference holds
for both:

parallel_retrieve_cursor/corner
  Two lines of the port's.  pg_cursors is PostgreSQL 19's view, with no
  is_parallel column, which an extension cannot add; and a session of the
  coordinator's own (-1U), an ordinary one here, declares a parallel
  retrieve cursor, which Cloudberry's utility mode refuses.

parallel_retrieve_cursor/explain
  ORCA's text of the plans Cloudberry's planner makes: a column named with
  its relation (Output: t1.a), a function's scan with no alias and its
  filter above it in a Subquery Scan -- where the endpoint is the
  coordinator's either way -- and PostgreSQL 19's pg_class columns in place
  of Cloudberry's.  Where the endpoints are, which the test is for, is
  Cloudberry's in every case: on all 3 segments, on segments [1, 0], on the
  one segment a replicated table is read on, on the coordinator.

parallel_retrieve_cursor/status_check, parallel_retrieve_cursor/status_wait
  One thing of the port's dispatcher, not of the endpoints: a segment that
  restarts after its endpoint's sender was killed with SIGQUIT (Test4) fails
  the coordinator's statements in PostgreSQL's words for a connection that
  closed, where Cloudberry's add "Error on receive from" the segment.
